\title{Tokalator: A Token Budget Monitor and Cost Estimator\\for AI-Assisted Software Development}

\author{
  Vahid Faraji \\
  \texttt{vfaraji89@gmail.com}
}

\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Large language models used in code editors process context through fixed-size token windows.
Developers who use AI assistants such as GitHub Copilot or Claude Code often have no
visibility into how much of that window their open files, instructions, and conversation
history consume. Tokalator is an open-source toolkit that makes token usage visible and
actionable. It consists of three components: a VS Code extension with a real-time sidebar
dashboard, an MCP server that brings token counting into Claude Code, and a web platform
with cost calculators and economic analysis tools. This paper describes the system
architecture, the relevance scoring algorithm, and the cost model. We show a step-by-step
usage example and discuss how the tool helps developers reduce unnecessary spending on
AI API calls.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}

When a developer asks an AI coding assistant to fix a bug or write a function, the assistant
reads the open files, the conversation history, and any attached instructions. All of this
text is converted into tokens before the model processes it. Each model has a maximum number
of tokens it can handle at once, called the context window. For example, Claude Opus 4.6
has a context window of 200,000 tokens.

The problem is that developers cannot see how many tokens their current session uses. A
developer with 30 open tabs may not realize that those files alone consume 60\% of the
context window, leaving little room for the model to produce a useful response. Worse, every
