}

\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Large language models used in code editors process context through fixed-size token windows.
Developers who use AI assistants such as GitHub Copilot or Claude Code often have no
visibility into how much of that window their open files, instructions, and conversation
history consume. Tokalator is an open-source toolkit that makes token usage visible and
actionable. It consists of three components: a VS Code extension with a real-time sidebar
dashboard, an MCP server that brings token counting into Claude Code, and a web platform
with cost calculators and economic analysis tools. This paper describes the system
architecture, the relevance scoring algorithm, and the cost model. We show a step-by-step
usage example and discuss how the tool helps developers reduce unnecessary spending on
AI API calls.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}

When a developer asks an AI coding assistant to fix a bug or write a function, the assistant
reads the open files, the conversation history, and any attached instructions. All of this
text is converted into tokens before the model processes it. Each model has a maximum number
of tokens it can handle at once, called the context window. For example, Claude Opus 4.6
has a context window of 200,000 tokens.

The problem is that developers cannot see how many tokens their current session uses. A
developer with 30 open tabs may not realize that those files alone consume 60\% of the
context window, leaving little room for the model to produce a useful response. Worse, every
token sent to the API has a dollar cost. Teams that run hundreds of AI-assisted sessions per
day can accumulate significant bills without understanding where the money goes.

Tokalator solves this problem. The name combines ``token'' and ``calculator,'' inspired by the
abacus as a simple counting tool. It gives developers three things: (1) a live count of how
many tokens their current editor state uses, (2) a relevance score for each open file so they
know which tabs matter and which waste budget, and (3) a cost estimate based on current
provider pricing.


\section{System Architecture}

Tokalator has three independent components that share a common core logic.

\subsection{VS Code Extension}

The extension adds a sidebar panel to VS Code that updates in real time. When a developer
opens, closes, or edits a file, the extension recalculates the token count and the budget
breakdown. It also provides a chat participant (\texttt{@tokalator}) that responds to
commands such as \texttt{/count}, \texttt{/breakdown}, and \texttt{/optimize}.

The extension uses the real Claude BPE tokenizer from the Anthropic SDK for Claude models
and the \texttt{o200k\_base} tokenizer from \texttt{js-tiktoken} for OpenAI models. For
Google models, it applies a heuristic of approximately one token per four characters.

\subsection{MCP Server}

The Model Context Protocol (MCP) server exposes four tools over stdio JSON-RPC:
\texttt{count\_tokens}, \texttt{estimate\_budget}, \texttt{preview\_turn}, and
\texttt{list\_models}. When registered in the \texttt{.mcp.json} configuration file, Claude
Code loads these tools automatically. A developer can then ask Claude Code to count tokens
or estimate the budget without leaving the terminal.

\subsection{Web Platform}

The web application, built with Next.js, provides interactive calculators for token cost
estimation, caching ROI analysis, and model comparison. It also hosts an economic analysis
tool based on the Cobb-Douglas quality function from Bergemann, Bonatti, and
Smolin~\cite{bergemann2025}.


\section{Relevance Scoring}

Not all open files are equally useful to the AI assistant. Tokalator assigns each open tab a
relevance score $R \in [0, 1]$ using five weighted signals:

\begin{equation}
R = w_l \cdot S_l + w_i \cdot S_i + w_p \cdot S_p + w_r \cdot S_r + w_d \cdot S_d
\end{equation}

\noindent where the signals and their default weights are:

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{5.5cm}@{}}
\toprule
Signal & Weight & Description \\
\midrule
Language match ($S_l$)  & 0.25 & 1 if the tab shares the same programming language as the active file, 0 otherwise \\
Import relation ($S_i$) & 0.30 & 1 if the active file imports or requires the tab file \\
Path similarity ($S_p$) & 0.20 & Ratio of shared directory prefix segments to total depth \\
Edit recency ($S_r$)    & 0.15 & 1 if edited within the last 2 minutes, decays over time \\
Diagnostics ($S_d$)     & 0.10 & 1 if the tab has compiler errors or warnings \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Relevance scoring signals and default weights.}
\label{tab:weights}
\end{table}

Pinned files always receive a score of 1.0 regardless of the computed value. The active
editor tab also receives a score of 1.0. When the user runs the \texttt{/optimize} command,
the extension identifies tabs with low relevance scores and suggests closing them to free
budget.


\section{Cost Model}

Tokalator computes API cost from four token categories: input, output, cache write, and
cache read. For a given model with per-million-token prices $c_{in}$, $c_{out}$,
$c_{cw}$, and $c_{cr}$, the total cost of a single API call is:

\begin{equation}
C = \frac{T_{in} \cdot c_{in} + T_{out} \cdot c_{out} + T_{cw} \cdot c_{cw} + T_{cr} \cdot c_{cr}}{10^6}
\end{equation}

The web platform extends this with a Cobb-Douglas quality function
$Q = X^{\alpha} \cdot Y^{\beta} \cdot (b + Z)^{\gamma}$, where $X$ is input tokens, $Y$ is
output tokens, $Z$ is cache tokens, $b$ is a base quality parameter, and
$\alpha, \beta, \gamma$ are sensitivity exponents. This model, drawn from recent work on the
economics of large language models~\cite{bergemann2025}, lets users explore the tradeoff
between spending more tokens and getting higher quality responses.


